Millennials will soon be the largest  generation in America's electorate with  potentially dictums dramatic effects on  the political landscape we're teaming up  this week with digital media company  refinery29 to explore the impact of  millennial women on the upcoming midterm  elections as part of our my vote series  we polled women across the country with  a focus on those issues between the ages  of 18 and 35 34 percent identified as  Democrats 27 percent as independents and  19 percent as Republicans the remaining  20 percent aren't sure of how they  identified politically we found that  seven in 10 are dissatisfied or angry  about the Trump presidency and the  direction the country is headed in but  only 30 percent of them say they will  definitely vote in November  with us now our refinery29 chief content  officer Amy Emmerich and CBS News  elections and surveys director Anthony  salvano great to see you both so I don't  like this statistic at all Amy young  women are the least likely to vote in  November why is that well that's the  goal is for us to use media and change  that right but I mean I believe I think  that the activism isn't necessarily  translating into the poll and one of the  reasons is in volunteerism and the  activism in the protesting we've seen  that's an experiential movement they can  feel the change they don't believe that  their vote matters right now they don't  trust the process they believe it's all  about politics and not about the policy  so why would you vote in something that  you necessarily don't believe in when  you can get out on the street and feel  the impact that you can make and yet  they're so invested in these policies  and making change specifically when it  comes to health care  why is health care such an important  issue for millennial women going into  the midterms well traditionally women  cared more about the health care in the  household right they did more doctor  visits with their kids they did more  doctor visits with their parents and as  women we have a long-standing  relationship with our gynecologists  starting at the age of 13 so we actually  go to the doctor more than men and  through that we see the discrepancies in  the system so they're much more focused  and they can see the issues and the  process at large you know it's  interesting Anthony the repercussions of  the 2014 midterm elections are such that  people now understand your vote  matter what is it specifically about  these younger women that they choose not  to but what reasons that they give well  look for many years we have seen young  people less likely to say that they're  going to vote you know part of that is  just as you grow older you start to see  how politics affects your life right or  you put down roots you buy a house you  have kids you start to see how policies  affect you so part of that is typical  but we also see here that further folks  the young women who say that they are  going to vote the motivation is really  high we find enthusiasm to be really  high we say that they think they think  that it matters who controls Congress as  opposed to the folks who say that they  are not going to vote and they say that  it doesn't matter who controls Commerce  so there are big differences there but  the motivation among those who do say  they're engaged is really really high  Amy what about actual participation in  terms of running for office we found  that a quarter of Republican women in  this age group say that women in office  would make things worse what explains  that well there's also the positive side  of that which they 50 percent thought  that it would be better to have women  more women in office right when you  combine the Democrats and the  Republicans I think when you look at the  Republican number there's something  behind this idea of feminism in this  belief that more women in office equals  feminism and what is their definition of  feminism and the study kind of showed us  that the Republican belief is that  feminism is where we blame men right  issues whereas the Democrat idea of  feminism is much more about equality and  I think that there's something to that  nuance that's leaning I wonder how much  the Clinton candidacy enshrined that  sort of part is a difference around  feminism and femininity Anthony yet  we've seen differences in this poll and  in others where we asked would you vote  for a man or woman if you had the choice  and Democrats are more likely to say  that they would pick the woman over the  man Republicans much less so and the  reason that they give when we ask them  is they think it would add diversity and  that's something that we have seen  Democrats respond to much more than  Republican voters who also by the way  overall tend to be older and not  necessarily part of this this generation  we're focusing on it's interesting  because every day we talk about how  polarized our society is right now but  among  lineal women at least 26 for only 26%  said that parties affiliation mattered  to them when it came to their candidate  of choice  are they being honest or we think  something different here we are seeing  something different and this is  something that defines this generation  not only are more of them saying that  they're independent but we also see that  as they get older they're not picking a  party  sometimes as folks get older they start  they pick one or the other this  generation is not and that's something  that is really defining for our folks  not be a bad thing for cultural  identification as much more than  anything else their self identifying in  a much more complex way in an  intersectional way than any other  generation before so you've got to  understand they care about culture and  values much more than they care about  party affiliate need to get out and vote  politicians need to figure out how to  get them out yeah it's a fascinating  sequence American voter Anthony  southmont oh and Amy Emmerich thank you  guys both
 

